89
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:38,200
PASSAGE 2
I don’t want to boast or anything, but I’ve always considered myself something of an elite sleeper.

90
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:45,400
Given the opportunity, I will sleep for marathon stretches and can doze through the most extreme situations.

91
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:59,580
On one very rough ferry crossing on the route to the Isles of Scilly, for example, my travelling companions spent the entire three-hour ride throwing up in the bathroom while I dozed happily on a plastic chair.

92
00:10:59,580 --> 00:11:05,100
Unfortunately, it has come to my attention that I am not an elite sleeper after all.

93
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:07,300
It seems I am just lazy.

94
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:18,220
Because elite sleepers are defined as the approximately 3% of the population who are biologically programmed to need less sleep than the rest of us.

95
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:31,580
According to a study that came out in March, elite sleepers have rare genetic changes, which means they can sleep fewer hours than mere mortals without any risk of cognitive decline.

96
00:11:31,580 --> 00:11:37,900
It may not be possible to change your own genes, but can you train yourself to need less sleep?

97
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:43,260
Is there a non-biological way to reach elite sleeper status?

98
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:47,300
I have spent the past year trying to answer that question.

99
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:56,560
Not for fun, I should add, but because having a baby has severely disrupted my sleep, for which I still have a great passion.

100
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:03,640
For a while, I assumed I’d be forced to become one of those people who jump out of bed at the crack of dawn.

101
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:19,760
After a year of tough scientific study, however, I have discovered being forced to get up early in the morning is very different from being an early bird.

102
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:38,760
Q12. What does the speaker say she did on her ride to the Isles of Scilly?

103
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:57,120
Q13. What do we learn from the passage about elite sleepers?

104
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:16,260
Q14. What has the speaker been trying to find out over the past year?

105
00:13:16,260 --> 00:13:59,720
Q15. What has the speaker discovered after a year of tough scientific study?

106
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:07,500
RECORDING 1
If you read an article about a controversial issue, do you think you’d realise if it had changed your beliefs?

107
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:14,360
No one knows your own mind like you do — it seems obvious that you would know if your beliefs had shifted.

108
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:30,880
And yet a new paper in The Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggests that we actually have very poor “awareness” of our own belief change, meaning that we will tend to underestimate how much we’ve been swayed by a convincing article.

109
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:42,100
The researchers recruited over 200 undergraduates across two studies and focused on their beliefs about whether physical punishment of kids is an effective form of discipline.

110
00:14:42,100 --> 00:14:53,300
The students reported their initial beliefs about whether physical punishment is an effective way to discipline a child on a scale from 1 (completely disbelieve) to 9 (completely believe).

111
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:59,560
Several weeks later, they were given one of two research-based texts to read.

112
00:14:59,560 --> 00:15:07,620
Each was several pages long, and either presented the arguments and data in favor of physical punishment or against it.

113
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:13,980
After this, the students answered some questions to test their comprehension and memory of the text.

114
00:15:13,980 --> 00:15:20,720
Then, the students again scored their belief in whether physical punishment is effective or not.

115
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:27,400
Finally, the researchers asked them to recall what their belief had been at the start of the study.

116
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:34,740
The students’ belief about physical punishment changed when they read a text that argued against their own initial position.

117
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:46,180
Crucially, the memory of their initial belief was shifted in the direction of their new belief — in fact, their memory was closer to their current belief than their original belief.

118
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:56,910
The more their belief had changed, the larger this memory bias tended to be, suggesting the students were relying on their current belief to deduce their initial belief.

119
00:15:56,910 --> 00:16:09,630
The memory bias was unrelated to the measures of how well they’d understood or recalled the text, suggesting these factors didn’t play a role in memory of initial belief or awareness of belief change.

120
00:16:09,630 --> 00:16:15,830
The researchers conceded that this research was about changes to mostly moderate beliefs.

121
00:16:15,830 --> 00:16:22,570
It’s likely the findings would be different in the context of changes to extreme or deeply held beliefs.

122
00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:48,210
However, our beliefs on most topics are in the moderate range, and as we go about our daily lives reading informative material, these intriguing findings suggest we are mostly ignorant of how what we just read has updated and altered our own position.

123
00:16:48,210 --> 00:17:08,670
Q16. What does a new paper in The Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology suggest?

124
00:17:08,670 --> 00:17:28,810
Q17. What happened when the students read a text that argued against their own initial position?

125
00:17:28,810 --> 00:17:47,450
Q18. What did the researchers concede concerning their findings?

126
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:53,250
RECORDING 2
As the American population grows, so does the number of American moms.

127
00:17:53,250 --> 00:18:05,750
But, more than a century after Mother’s Day became an official holiday, even as that number increases, the share of the American population who are mothers is at the lowest point in a quarter century.

128
00:18:05,750 --> 00:18:18,560
It’s frequently noted that fertility rates are falling sharply in richer countries, but the less observed consequence of this trend is that a decline in births can also mean a decline in motherhood in general.

129
00:18:18,560 --> 00:18:29,060
According to my analysis of data from the Census Bureau, the decline of American motherhood is real, occurring very quickly, and may continue for some time yet.

130
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:38,880
Not only are moms making up less of the population, but their characteristics are changing too, and in a way that might be linked to their proportional decline.

131
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:41,540
Moms today tend to be older than in the past.

132
00:18:41,540 --> 00:18:48,120
Just looking at recent years, the change in age-specific birth rates has been drastic.

133
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:59,920
In just the past few years, the peak childbearing age range for American women has advanced from that of 25-to-29 to that of 30-to-34.

134
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:09,660
Meanwhile, childbearing among women under 20 has fallen by half or more, while childbearing among women 35 and older is rising.

135
00:19:09,660 --> 00:19:18,220
One positive consequence of this age shift is that a larger proportion of new mothers are economically prepared to raise children.

136
00:19:18,220 --> 00:19:25,680
Less positively, however, many women find that, as they age, they can’t have as many kids as they would like.

137
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:30,380
Plus, having children later in life can increase the risk of health complications.

138
00:19:30,380 --> 00:19:40,660
These finer points aside, one major consequence of the older-moms trend is that fewer years of a woman’s life are spent as a mother.

139
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:51,320
This means that at any given time, a larger share of women and thus of the whole population will report not having children in government surveys.

140
00:19:51,320 --> 00:19:55,900
In other words, later motherhood means less motherhood.

141
00:19:55,900 --> 00:20:01,100
Even as motherhood rates decline, Mother’s Day, of course, will endure.

142
00:20:01,100 --> 00:20:07,560
In fact, despite the demographic shift, retail spending on the holiday appears to be rising.

143
00:20:07,560 --> 00:20:14,840
It is hard to say if Mother’s Day spending is rising more than one would expect given that the American population keeps growing.

144
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:36,620
But one factor might be that the proportion of women who are the mothers of adult children is rising, and those adult children may spend more generously when it comes to celebrating the moms they no longer live with.

145
00:20:36,620 --> 00:20:55,680
Q19. What does the speaker conclude from her analysis of the Census Bureau’s data?

146
00:20:55,680 --> 00:21:15,680
Q20. What does the speaker say is a positive consequence of the age shift in childbearing?

147
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:35,520
Q21. What might be one explanation for the rising retail spending on Mother’s Day?

148
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:48,960
RECORDING 3
Since NASA published a paper in 1989 claiming that houseplants can soak up pollution and toxic chemicals, businesses and homeowners have increasingly invested in greenery to help clean their air.

149
00:21:48,960 --> 00:22:00,520
But a new analysis suggests it could actually take more than 1,000 plants per square meter to gain a benefit any greater than simply opening a couple of windows.

150
00:22:00,520 --> 00:22:10,380
The problem lies in the fact that NASA conducted their test in sealed containers that do not simulate the conditions in most people’s homes or offices.

151
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:27,440
The space agency was primarily concerned about keeping the air fresh for astronauts’ cut-off in biosphere or space stations and helping to combat sick building syndrome, which had become a problem due to the super-insulated and energy-efficient offices of the late 1970s.

152
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:39,660
By the early 1980s, workers regularly complained of skin rashes, sleepiness, headaches, and allergies as they breathed in toxic chemicals from paints and plastics.

153
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:49,260
NASA found that certain plants could remove chemicals from the air, and even today, garden centers recommend the plants for air-cleaning properties.

154
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:58,140
However, a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years found that houseplants in a normal environment have little impact.

155
00:22:58,140 --> 00:23:03,020
In fact, natural ventilation is far better at cleaning the air.

156
00:23:03,020 --> 00:23:22,460
The researchers also calculated the clean air delivery rate for plants in the studies they analyzed and found that the rate at which plants dispersed the compounds was well below the usual rate of air exchange in a normal building caused by the movements of people coming and going, opening doors and windows.

157
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:35,140
Many of the studies did show a reduction in the concentration of volatile organic compounds over time, which is likely why people have seized on them to praise the air-purifying virtues of plants.

158
00:23:35,140 --> 00:23:52,000
But the research’s calculation showed it would take 10 to 1,000 plants per square meter of floor space to compete with the air-cleaning capacity of a building’s air-handling system or even just a couple of open windows in a house.

159
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:58,620
In contrast, NASA’s sealed experiment recommended one potted plant per 100 square feet.

160
00:23:58,620 --> 00:24:05,840
This is certainly an example of how scientific findings can be misleading or misinterpreted over time.

161
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:25,880
But it’s also a great example of how scientific research should continually reexamine and question findings to get closer to the ground truth of understanding what’s actually happening.

162
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:45,780
Q22. What does NASA’s 1989 paper claim houseplants can do?

163
00:24:45,780 --> 00:25:06,240
Q23. What is said to be the problem with NASA’S study reported in its 1989 paper?

164
00:25:06,240 --> 00:25:26,880
Q24. What is the finding of a new evaluation of dozens of studies spanning 30 years?

165
00:25:26,880 --> 00:25:49,793
Q25. What does NASA’s sealed experiment recommendation exemplify in scientists’ pursuit of truth?
